{
  "row": {
    "id": 13,
    "scenario": "Save and then immediately ask; manual edit or delete; an external new file",
    "required_outcome": "Read-after-write holds; stale selected files are suppressed; the new file appears by the next scan, with freshness reported"
  },
  "design": ["§5 Freshness without a watcher: service writes update the index synchronously; reconciliation is a stat scan; verification re-hashes every selected file; a missed new external file may be absent until the next scan", "§5 step 7: a dirty or deleted note is never served from cached text", "§4: an external edit downgrades a note to `external`", "§7a memory_changed with change: external"],
  "loads_in": ["slice-2", "slice-3"],
  "notes_for_implementer": "Steps 1–3: a service store is searchable and recallable at once, `freshness: verified`, `memory_used` names it. Steps 4–5: an edit behind the service's back, with no scan in between — verification (step 7 of §5) re-hashes the selected file, so the result carries the current bytes (the appended sentence) and never the indexed text, and the note reads as `external`. Steps 6–7: a deletion behind the service's back is not served at all, from either path. Steps 8–10: a hand-written new file is allowed to be absent before the scan (`ids_may_include`), must be present after it, origin `external`, and the scan publishes one `memory_changed` per detected change — with no `surface`, because §7a's enum is `user | model_tool | extraction` and a scan is none of them (P13; the field is omitted, not invented, until the design adds a value or makes it optional). Step 11: the stat scan is mtime+size+inode, so a rewrite with identical bytes changes nothing. Slice 2 runs every step through `memory_index` + `memory_files` with the partition given.",
